            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.107,
            0.821,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "(iv) $\\left( { \\frac { 3 } { 4 } } s   +   8 t \\right) ^ { 2 }$ (v) $\\left( X   +   { \\frac { 1 } { 2 y } } \\right) ^ { 2 }$ (vi) $\\left( \\frac{1}{x}  +  \\frac{1}{y} \\right)^2$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.172,
            0.788,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": "2. Using the same identity, find the values of the following: (i) $( 6 4 ) ^ { 2 }$ (ii) (105)<sup>2</sup> (iii) (205)<sup>2</sup>"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.249,
            0.822,
            0.296
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.3 Factorisation of Algebraic Expressions Using Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.307,
            0.886,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": "The identity $(a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ab + b^{2}$ can also be used to find factors of some algebraic expressions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.355,
            0.769,
            0.376
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5:** Consider the algebraic expression $x ^ { 2 } + 4 x + 4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.384,
            0.334,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "We observe that"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.412,
            0.481,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "$x ^ { 2 } = ( x ) ^ { 2 } ,   4 = 2 ^ { 2 }$ and 4x = 2(2)(x)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.44,
            0.882,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "Hence, $x^{2}+4x+4=x^{2}+2(x)(2)+2^{2}$ can be compared with $a ^ { 2 }   +   2 ( a ) ( b )   +   b ^ { 2 } ,$ where a = x and b = 2."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.488,
            0.884,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "We conclude that $x^{2}+4x+4=(x+2)^{2}$ . Therefore, we can say that $( x + 2 )$ is a factor of $x^{2} + 4x \\pm 4.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.533,
            0.883,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6:** Let us try to find factors of another algebraic expression: 36x<sup>2</sup> + 12x + 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.589,
            0.624,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "Writing this in the form $a^{2} \\pm 2ab + b^{2}$ we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.617,
            0.53,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "36x^{2}+12x+1=(6x)^{2}+2(6x)(1)+1^{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.646,
            0.785,
            0.666
          ],
          "content": "where a = 6x and b = 1. Therefore, $36x^{2} + 12x + 1 = (6x + 1)^{2}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.673,
            0.592,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "Thus (6x + 1) is a factor of $36x^{2} + 12x + 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.701,
            0.884,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7:** Let us try to factor $5 0 p ^ { 2 } \\pm 6 0 p q \\pm 1 8 q ^ { 2 }$ . What will a and b be in this case?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.751,
            0.886,
            0.81
          ],
          "content": "Try to think of a term whose square is $5 0 p ^ { 2 }$ . It is $\\sqrt { 5 0 } p$ . But if we want to avoid using the square root symbol we may proceed as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.817,
            0.688,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "We observe that 2 is a common factor of the terms"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.844,
            0.324,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": "50p<sup>2</sup>, 60pq, 18q<sup>2</sup>."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.87,
            0.639,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "Thus $5 0 p ^ { 2 } + 6 0 p q + 1 8 q ^ { 2 } = 2 ( 2 5 p ^ { 2 } + 3 0 p q + 9 q ^ { 2 } ) .$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
